\subsection{\code{BaseRPointLayerProcessor$legend_group_layers()}}{
  One point layer per colour the plot's legend names

\code{plot(x, y, col = colours[group])} with a \code{legend()} naming the colours
is how Base R draws groups of points. The layer was read as one cloud,
each point carrying its colour's name in a \code{color} field maidr.js does
not read, so a reader never heard which group a point was in and could
not walk one group at a time. When the legend drawn on the same plot
names every colour the points are drawn in, each colour is now a layer
of its own, in the legend's order, named for its entry and the
legend's title (\code{group_layer_name()}), as a grouped lattice \code{xyplot()}
is read. Points of a colour the legend does not name, or a plot with
no legend, are read as before: a colour alone names no group.

R draws the points in one grob, in the order given, and the export
numbers each \verb{<use>} by its place in it, so a group's points are named
by those numbers.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRPointLayerProcessor$legend_group_layers(
  layer_info,
  points,
  selectors,
  axes,
  title
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Layer information with the recorded call and its plot
group}
      \item{\code{points}}{The layer's points, from \code{extract_data()}}
      \item{\code{selectors}}{The layer's selectors, from \code{generate_selectors()}}
      \item{\code{axes}}{The layer's axes}
      \item{\code{title}}{The chart's title}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A multi-layer result, or NULL when no legend groups the points
into two groups or more
  }
}

